From 2c0d5e3aa75bc963141264990a8f8fa58061fd4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 21:49:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Wire resolve_tier_conflict into RegulatoryDeck: cross-lens
 tier conflicts

resolve_tier_conflict (regulatory_checks.py) existed and was tested in
isolation but nothing ever called it -- two live regulatory lenses
disagreeing about the same input variable's authorization tier just
silently produced two separate findings, with no signal that they
disagreed at all.

RegulatoryDeck now detects this structurally: any finding carrying
both a "variable" and a "tier" key in its evidence is treated as a
tier claim, regardless of which lens or which check produced it (not
a match on check name -- proven by the test fixture using a
deliberately different check name than CFPB's). When two or more
DIFFERENT live lenses hold conflicting tier claims for the same
variable on the same episode, they're folded pairwise through
resolve_tier_conflict to find the resolved (stricter) tier, and a
single cross_lens_tier_conflict finding is raised -- always
ACTION_FLAG, disclosed to the ledger like any other live finding
(fail-closed, before judgment returns), and attributed to whichever
lens already held the resolved tier. The other lens's claim is
superseded on the record, not silently dropped.

Wired into both judge() (disclosed after all per-lens findings are
already on the chain, before any block takes effect) and explain()
(reporting-only, same as every other regulatory finding there --
never writes).

New test fixture _StrictStateTierLens (Tests/test_regulatory_cassettes.py)
models a second jurisdiction that explicitly prohibits (T0) an input
CFPB's reference lens has no declared tier for at all (T5_UNDECLARED
once its tier screen is enabled) -- a real disagreement between two
independently-authored lenses, not a contrived one. 6 new tests:
conflict detected and resolved to the actually-stricter tier (asserted
against resolve_tier_conflict itself, not a hardcoded literal),
disclosed under the winning lens's identity and verifies in the chain,
no conflict when only one lens claims a variable, no conflict when
lenses agree, conflict finding never escalates to a block, and
explain() includes it while writing nothing.

Full suite: 442 passed, 6 skipped (436 baseline + 6 new), run twice
back-to-back with zero DB reset in between. ruff (pinned 0.15.22) and
bandit -ll both clean.
